WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.201
This briefing comes from Book 13 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook, Governance,

2
00:00:05.201 --> 00:00:09.174
Drift and Evidence: why compliance
decays the day after the audit,

3
00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:11.087
and the governance that doesn't.

4
00:00:11.087 --> 00:00:15.093
I'll take four questions: why a
signed package starts aging at once,

5
00:00:15.093 --> 00:00:19.024
what keeps governance true between
assessments, who governs it,

6
00:00:19.024 --> 00:00:23.326
and how to run it at machine tempo,
reuse it across agencies and scale it.

7
00:00:23.326 --> 00:00:27.657
The short version: keep one authoritative
record, let the systems prove

8
00:00:27.657 --> 00:00:32.410
they still match it, and keep people
on the decisions only people can make.

9
00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.222
Picture the week an
authorization package is signed.

10
00:00:36.222 --> 00:00:38.122
On that day the package is true.

11
00:00:38.122 --> 00:00:42.833
The next morning an administrator
changes a firewall rule to fix an outage,

12
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:47.998
a contractor's account outlives the contract,
a cloud provider ships a new default.

13
00:00:47.998 --> 00:00:49.772
None of it reaches the package.

14
00:00:49.772 --> 00:00:53.273
Federal guidance already
points away from the snapshot.

15
00:00:53.273 --> 00:00:57.948
FISMA sets a floor: test at least
annually, with automated tools.

16
00:00:57.948 --> 00:01:03.385
There's no federal three-year rule; the
authorization's term is the agency's to set.

17
00:01:03.385 --> 00:01:08.172
NIST defines continuous monitoring as
ongoing awareness, not a calendar.

18
00:01:08.172 --> 00:01:12.659
The Risk Management Framework calls
reauthorization the static, single

19
00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:17.734
point-in-time decision, and asks agencies
to make ongoing authorization a priority.

20
00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:23.047
OMB's Circular A-130 tells them to
make that transition when eligible.

21
00:01:23.047 --> 00:01:25.186
So why do so few systems get there?

22
00:01:25.186 --> 00:01:28.358
NIST answers it: without
substantial automation,

23
00:01:28.358 --> 00:01:31.685
ongoing authorization isn't
feasible or practical.

24
00:01:31.685 --> 00:01:36.435
And adding checkboxes makes reporting
heavier while leaving the change untouched.

25
00:01:36.435 --> 00:01:38.690
That's the compliance paradox.

26
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.815
The mechanism has a name: drift.

27
00:01:41.815 --> 00:01:44.807
Drift is any difference
between what the authoritative

28
00:01:44.807 --> 00:01:47.927
record says should be true and
what the running estate does.

29
00:01:47.927 --> 00:01:52.581
A setting changed and never recorded,
an account that outlived its owner,

30
00:01:52.581 --> 00:01:55.140
a rule that quietly stopped evaluating.

31
00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.096
NIST names the sources:
unauthorized change,

32
00:01:58.096 --> 00:02:03.368
from attackers or from honest errors by
authorized staff; changes made outside

33
00:02:03.368 --> 00:02:07.123
change control; provider
changes that are hard to detect;

34
00:02:07.123 --> 00:02:09.040
staff turnover; and upgrades.

35
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.452
The program ranks drift first among
governance threats for three reasons.

36
00:02:13.452 --> 00:02:16.764
It's continuous, a gradient
rather than an event.

37
00:02:16.764 --> 00:02:21.150
It's silent: the directory reports
the rule as configured and the console

38
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:25.315
reports the policy as assigned,
because each reports what it was told.

39
00:02:25.315 --> 00:02:27.465
And it's the opening other threats use.

40
00:02:27.465 --> 00:02:31.079
NSA and CISA called the most
common misconfigurations

41
00:02:31.079 --> 00:02:33.540
their teams find systemic weaknesses.

42
00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.681
NIST's controls describe drift
detection without the word: a baseline,

43
00:02:37.681 --> 00:02:42.330
controlled change, approved deviations,
and someone who keeps checking.

44
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.505
Drift can only be measured
against something.

45
00:02:45.505 --> 00:02:48.979
For twenty years many enterprises
kept their governance,

46
00:02:48.979 --> 00:02:51.367
unwritten, in the Active Directory tree.

47
00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:53.729
A flat cloud directory doesn't carry it,

48
00:02:53.729 --> 00:02:58.081
so the program writes the governance
down, in one authoritative record.

49
00:02:58.081 --> 00:03:02.399
The record is a version-controlled
repository of policies,

50
00:03:02.399 --> 00:03:04.867
plans, evidence, schemas and rules.

51
00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:06.979
It doesn't master every fact.

52
00:03:06.979 --> 00:03:11.568
HR owns who a worker is; the identity
platform issues credentials.

53
00:03:11.568 --> 00:03:16.930
The record holds the authority: which system
owns each fact, and who may write it.

54
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.630
Runtime systems read
it and never write it.

55
00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:23.562
Private copies are forbidden,
because a local copy someone reads

56
00:03:23.562 --> 00:03:27.130
because the repository was slow
is a second source of truth.

57
00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:32.143
Every derived report carries its
source, version, time and producer.

58
00:03:32.143 --> 00:03:34.574
The record covers six control planes,

59
00:03:34.574 --> 00:03:39.204
each authoritative for one domain, so
every finding has one owning team.

60
00:03:39.204 --> 00:03:44.630
NIST's CM-2 baseline is this record,
kept in a form machines can compare.

61
00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.355
A record is only as good
as the path into it.

62
00:03:48.355 --> 00:03:52.305
Most drift isn't malicious;
it's a hotfix during an outage.

63
00:03:52.305 --> 00:03:57.559
So the program allows one path for every
operational change, whether it moves,

64
00:03:57.559 --> 00:04:02.730
adds, changes, deletes or resets something,
and no second path for urgent work.

65
00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.593
The path has five clauses.

66
00:04:04.593 --> 00:04:06.817
Originate from the source of truth.

67
00:04:06.817 --> 00:04:10.718
Authorize from the authority
model, not from whoever is nearby.

68
00:04:10.718 --> 00:04:14.442
Execute under least privilege,
elevated just in time.

69
00:04:14.442 --> 00:04:17.268
Close with the verification
that justified it.

70
00:04:17.268 --> 00:04:19.556
And emit the evidence automatically.

71
00:04:19.556 --> 00:04:22.437
Skip a clause and the
operation isn't a change

72
00:04:22.437 --> 00:04:24.930
with a missing form; it's a drift finding.

73
00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:29.184
Changes to the record travel as
reviewed pull requests: two people,

74
00:04:29.184 --> 00:04:33.592
a signed merge, and branch protection
that binds administrators too.

75
00:04:33.592 --> 00:04:37.017
Emergencies are back-ported
within one business day,

76
00:04:37.017 --> 00:04:39.118
and reported as drift until then.

77
00:04:39.118 --> 00:04:44.006
The identity that decides never acts,
and the one that acts never decides.

78
00:04:44.006 --> 00:04:47.330
And no closure counts unless
it carries its proof.

79
00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.430
When the program says a control is
satisfied, how does anyone know it's right?

80
00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.317
The usual answer is trust.

81
00:04:54.317 --> 00:04:59.133
The program's answer is determinism:
given the same version of the record and

82
00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:04.255
the same collected inputs, the platform
produces the same evidence, byte for byte.

83
00:05:04.255 --> 00:05:06.880
Then an assessor doesn't
have to trust the run.

84
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.305
They repeat it and compare the hashes.

85
00:05:09.305 --> 00:05:11.430
Automation isn't the same thing.

86
00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.328
Automation makes the answer arrive faster;

87
00:05:14.328 --> 00:05:18.030
determinism makes it the same
answer no matter who asks.

88
00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:21.992
The rules live in three layers,
each with its own reviewer.

89
00:05:21.992 --> 00:05:26.842
Rules say what exists and what it means,
and change only through governance review.

90
00:05:26.842 --> 00:05:29.867
Mappings say which rules
apply to which controls.

91
00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:34.942
Runtime code evaluates them, and is
the only layer allowed to change state.

92
00:05:34.942 --> 00:05:39.555
One limit: determinism is faithful
to the record, not a check on it.

93
00:05:39.555 --> 00:05:43.100
A wrong rule gives the same
wrong answer every time.

94
00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:47.752
The drift engine compares observed
state with a versioned record,

95
00:05:47.752 --> 00:05:49.525
not a last-known-good snapshot.

96
00:05:49.525 --> 00:05:53.413
An approved change updates the
record first, so it isn't drift.

97
00:05:53.413 --> 00:05:57.263
There's one drift model, with
five classes, each a question.

98
00:05:57.263 --> 00:05:59.500
Schema: is the shape still valid?

99
00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.837
Semantic: is the value still true?

100
00:06:01.837 --> 00:06:05.825
Provenance: does every change
trace to an authorized source?

101
00:06:05.825 --> 00:06:09.900
Authorization: did anything act
outside what it was authorized to do?

102
00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:13.400
Identity: does every object
resolve to the record?

103
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:18.212
The drift checks in the other books are
instances of these five, not separate lists.

104
00:06:18.212 --> 00:06:20.561
Severity is computed, not argued,

105
00:06:20.561 --> 00:06:24.301
from the class and whether
the claim is in active use.

106
00:06:24.301 --> 00:06:28.850
P1 halts and alerts a named role,
and is never fixed automatically.

107
00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:32.837
P2 has an hour, P3 a day, P4 three days.

108
00:06:32.837 --> 00:06:37.363
Severity sets the clock; the
actuation ladder decides who may act.

109
00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:42.518
And be precise with an assessor about what's
built: schema and provenance detection

110
00:06:42.518 --> 00:06:47.820
are built, semantic is partial, and
authorization and identity are still design.

111
00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:53.245
An attestation is a person signing that
a system was in a stated condition.

112
00:06:53.245 --> 00:06:57.495
It answers who is accountable, and
federal authorization needs that answer.

113
00:06:57.495 --> 00:07:01.945
Evidence answers a different question:
is the claim true, and can I check it?

114
00:07:01.945 --> 00:07:04.532
The program inverts the usual package.

115
00:07:04.532 --> 00:07:09.412
Evidence is the substance, and
attestation is the accountability wrapper:

116
00:07:09.412 --> 00:07:13.819
people sign for the rules, the
exceptions and the acceptance of risk.

117
00:07:13.819 --> 00:07:18.561
NIST's assessment guide says that when the
intended state is expressed as data and

118
00:07:18.561 --> 00:07:21.281
compared automatically
with the actual state,

119
00:07:21.281 --> 00:07:24.558
the output is equivalent to
control assessment results.

120
00:07:24.558 --> 00:07:29.321
The Risk Management Framework lets a tool's
report serve as the assessment report.

121
00:07:29.321 --> 00:07:32.219
And FedRAMP's 2026 rules tell assessors:

122
00:07:32.219 --> 00:07:35.908
don't simply review
documentation or screenshots.

123
00:07:35.908 --> 00:07:37.408
Be careful what you claim.

124
00:07:37.408 --> 00:07:39.495
A hash shows that something changed.

125
00:07:39.495 --> 00:07:42.195
A hash chain shows removal or reordering.

126
00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:44.520
Only a signature shows who produced it.

127
00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:50.330
And some controls, like a training record
or a contract clause, stay attestations.

128
00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.743
The program doesn't replace
the platforms an agency runs.

129
00:07:54.743 --> 00:07:57.010
It reaches each one through an adapter:

130
00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:00.980
a translator that reads the platform's
native state through its own

131
00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:05.304
interface and writes it into the
program's common record, with provenance.

132
00:08:05.304 --> 00:08:09.331
The rules, the drift engine and
the evidence chain see one record,

133
00:08:09.331 --> 00:08:11.230
whichever vendor produced it.

134
00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:14.493
Adapters hold no rules
and make no decisions.

135
00:08:14.493 --> 00:08:18.117
Adapters come in two kinds,
kept in separate lists.

136
00:08:18.117 --> 00:08:21.942
Conformance adapters are
read-only and sit at L1.

137
00:08:21.942 --> 00:08:28.143
Modernization adapters change state, only
on authorized plans, at L3 by default.

138
00:08:28.143 --> 00:08:32.954
Each declares one of five missions,
and every one inherits four invariants.

139
00:08:32.954 --> 00:08:36.130
Read-only comes first,
and it has to be verified.

140
00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:40.241
CISA's ScubaGear is read-only in
purpose, yet its documentation

141
00:08:40.241 --> 00:08:44.818
notes that a Global Administrator
always has the permissions it needs.

142
00:08:44.818 --> 00:08:49.531
So the program compares what an identity
is actually granted with what it declares.

143
00:08:49.531 --> 00:08:51.367
Lock-in doesn't disappear.

144
00:08:51.367 --> 00:08:56.750
It moves to small, replaceable adapters
and to a record the agency owns.

145
00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.888
None of this machinery decides anything.

146
00:08:59.888 --> 00:09:05.550
A drift finding asks: change the estate,
change the record, or carry the difference?

147
00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:10.313
Only people can answer the last two, so
the program names one decider for each.

148
00:09:10.313 --> 00:09:15.763
The steward keeps the record: its protections,
merge rights, checks and backups.

149
00:09:15.763 --> 00:09:21.275
The steward doesn't decide what the facts
mean; HR, application and data owners do.

150
00:09:21.275 --> 00:09:23.874
The CISO decides control requirements,

151
00:09:23.874 --> 00:09:27.850
even when they're written as code,
and decides on drift evidence.

152
00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:32.875
The governance board is the change
control element NIST's CM-3 calls for.

153
00:09:32.875 --> 00:09:36.168
It meets every two weeks and
reviews rather than votes,

154
00:09:36.168 --> 00:09:38.875
so every decision has
one accountable owner.

155
00:09:38.875 --> 00:09:43.193
The authorizing official, the only
official who can accept risk,

156
00:09:43.193 --> 00:09:48.000
signs every open-ended gap and every
class of change allowed to run at L4.

157
00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.314
A bounded exception, with an end
date and a compensating control,

158
00:09:52.314 --> 00:09:55.325
is the CISO's, and none
renews automatically.

159
00:09:55.325 --> 00:10:00.259
And the repository is a federal record:
the records officer decides retention,

160
00:10:00.259 --> 00:10:04.040
and history is never rewritten
without that decision.

161
00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:08.168
A platform that detects
perfectly and closes slowly

162
00:10:08.168 --> 00:10:11.315
produces an accurate list
of things nobody fixed.

163
00:10:11.315 --> 00:10:13.040
Tempo keeps that list short.

164
00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:17.383
The program declares a response
window for each severity:

165
00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:21.003
immediate for P1, then an
hour, a day and three days.

166
00:10:21.003 --> 00:10:26.923
External clocks win: a known exploited
vulnerability runs on BOD 26-04,

167
00:10:26.923 --> 00:10:30.802
and an incident runs on
CISA's one-hour notice.

168
00:10:30.802 --> 00:10:33.674
The usual ticket sends
a person to a console,

169
00:10:33.674 --> 00:10:36.227
and their reply of done is an assertion.

170
00:10:36.227 --> 00:10:40.336
The program's rule is that wherever
a machine can reach the surface,

171
00:10:40.336 --> 00:10:42.353
the ticket authorizes a machine.

172
00:10:42.353 --> 00:10:47.091
A person approves; the platform
executes from a versioned plan under

173
00:10:47.091 --> 00:10:51.664
a service identity, after a dry run,
and re-reads the object to verify.

174
00:10:51.664 --> 00:10:54.665
A class qualifies when
it passes six tests.

175
00:10:54.665 --> 00:10:59.540
At L3, the default, a named
approver releases each instance.

176
00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:03.790
At L4 the authorizing official
accepts the whole class in advance.

177
00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:08.490
Conditional Access and tenant-wide
role changes stay at L3 permanently.

178
00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:11.876
Where no machine can reach,
the fallback is a declared,

179
00:11:11.876 --> 00:11:16.250
recorded privileged session,
never a quiet trip to the console.

180
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.638
A platform that twenty agencies use is
examined by twenty assessment teams,

181
00:11:21.638 --> 00:11:25.025
producing twenty snapshots
at twenty different times.

182
00:11:25.025 --> 00:11:27.288
Reciprocity is the alternative.

183
00:11:27.288 --> 00:11:30.500
NIST defines it as accepting
each other's assessments.

184
00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:35.200
Nothing in it lets one authorizing
official accept risk for another agency.

185
00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:37.262
NIST gives three mechanisms.

186
00:11:37.262 --> 00:11:41.208
An authorization to use accepts
another federal entity's package,

187
00:11:41.208 --> 00:11:43.863
and the customer's
official carries the same

188
00:11:43.863 --> 00:11:47.450
responsibility as one issuing
an authorization to operate.

189
00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:52.237
A joint authorization has several officials
accept one system's risk together.

190
00:11:52.237 --> 00:11:56.391
For cloud services, OMB's
M-24-15 makes reuse the default:

191
00:11:56.391 --> 00:12:01.928
agencies presume a FedRAMP package
adequate, but only while continuous

192
00:12:01.928 --> 00:12:07.188
monitoring maintains it, and asking
for more needs a documented need.

193
00:12:07.188 --> 00:12:11.674
FedRAMP is plain that each agency
still authorizes its own systems.

194
00:12:11.674 --> 00:12:16.104
Its 46 Key Security Indicators
bind providers, not agencies;

195
00:12:16.104 --> 00:12:19.825
agencies review each
provider's quarterly report.

196
00:12:19.825 --> 00:12:24.244
So the program's old single-authorization
wording is corrected:

197
00:12:24.244 --> 00:12:29.030
one package, many decisions, each
agency signing for its own risk.

198
00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:32.280
A real agency doesn't have one estate.

199
00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:35.392
It has hundreds of systems
and several boundaries.

200
00:12:35.392 --> 00:12:40.706
NIST draws the line: each system's monitoring
strategy follows an organization-wide

201
00:12:40.706 --> 00:12:43.818
one, approved by the senior
accountable official

202
00:12:43.818 --> 00:12:46.779
for risk management, with
minimum frequencies.

203
00:12:46.779 --> 00:12:51.580
The program's scaling rule is to hold the
definitions small and let the uses grow.

204
00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:56.942
The record's core, the five drift classes
and the four severities stay fixed,

205
00:12:56.942 --> 00:13:00.055
so a P1 means the same
thing in every program.

206
00:13:00.055 --> 00:13:04.480
Adapters, mappings and evidence
grow, each validated on entry.

207
00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:07.195
Boundaries are recorded
as governed objects,

208
00:13:07.195 --> 00:13:09.767
so findings cite the
boundary they crossed.

209
00:13:09.767 --> 00:13:11.768
Coverage is reported honestly.

210
00:13:11.768 --> 00:13:16.118
Every control is covered,
partial, a gap, or out of scope.

211
00:13:16.118 --> 00:13:19.442
Every partial or gap enters
a register with an owner,

212
00:13:19.442 --> 00:13:22.456
the evidence that will
prove closure, and a date.

213
00:13:22.456 --> 00:13:26.049
It closes by configuration,
by a compensating control,

214
00:13:26.049 --> 00:13:31.450
or by the authorizing official's
acceptance, and closed items stay for audit.

215
00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:33.575
So, four decisions.

216
00:13:33.575 --> 00:13:37.136
Charter the governance board in
writing and name the steward,

217
00:13:37.136 --> 00:13:39.650
with one decider for
every kind of decision.

218
00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:44.936
Settle the record before the next migration:
which system owns each fact, the rules,

219
00:13:44.936 --> 00:13:47.424
and the provenance each value carries,

220
00:13:47.424 --> 00:13:51.388
so the migration changes who
supplies a fact, not what it means.

221
00:13:51.388 --> 00:13:56.881
Set the tempo: response windows by
severity, gated actuation at L3 by default,

222
00:13:56.881 --> 00:14:02.025
and the authorizing official's signature
on every class that runs at L4.

223
00:14:02.025 --> 00:14:04.507
And plan the move to
ongoing authorization,

224
00:14:04.507 --> 00:14:08.850
on evidence the systems produce rather
than attestations people assemble.

225
00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:11.050
The next step needs no procurement.

226
00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:14.864
Classify every control's
coverage, open the gap register,

227
00:14:14.864 --> 00:14:20.138
and record every drift finding in the same
contract shape, however it was found.

228
